# /etc/audit/rules.d/cmdscribe.rules
#
# MINIMAL by design. cmdscribe's shell layer already covers interactive
# commands; auditd is here for the two things a shell hook cannot do:
# tamper-proof attribution (auid) and guarding the shell hook itself.
#
# Deploy with: augenrules --load   (or: service auditd restart)
#
# The filters below were measured on Ubuntu 24.04, not copied from a template.
# Each one is load-bearing, and the comments say which way it fails.

-D
-b 8192
-f 1

# Every execve traceable to a human login. auid is stamped at login by
# pam_loginuid and is immutable for the session -- including across
# sudo, su - and pkexec. That is the only attribution that cannot be
# argued with. auid=unset is boot-time daemons: skipped.
#
# There is deliberately NO `auid>=1000` floor here. That filter is the usual
# incantation and it silently excludes every interactive user whose uid is below
# 1000. Measured on a host whose only human user has uid 501 (macOS-mapped, which
# is what Lima does and what NFS, LDAP and macOS-integrated fleets do too): the
# rule loaded, `auditctl -l` showed it, auditd ran, the kernel emitted records for
# the same user under other rules -- and `ausearch -k cmd` returned nothing. Not
# an error. An absence. In an auditing tool that is the worst possible failure,
# because "no records" reads as "nothing happened" rather than "we filtered them
# out". Accounts below 1000 are not exotic: RHEL <=6 put the first user at 500,
# and directory-backed fleets assign whatever they assign.
#
# If the volume is too high on a busy host, raise a floor DELIBERATELY and record
# that you did. Narrowing coverage should be a decision with a name on it, not a
# side effect of a copied rule.
-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -S execve                  -F auid!=unset -F auid!=0 -k cmd
-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -S execve                  -F auid=0                   -k cmd_root
-a exit,always -F arch=b32 -S execve                  -F auid!=unset -F auid!=0 -k cmd

# execve as well as execve. The shipped rules used to cover only execve, and
# execveat is not theoretical: /bin/true on this host produced an execveat record
# while no execve rule matched it. Anything reaching for a binary by file
# descriptor (fexecve, some loaders, some container runtimes) was invisible to the
# layer whose purpose is to see what the shell layer cannot.
-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -S execveat                -F auid!=unset -F auid!=0 -k cmd
-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -S execveat                -F auid=0                   -k cmd_root
-a exit,always -F arch=b32 -S execveat                -F auid!=unset -F auid!=0 -k cmd

# auditd watches the layer that is trivial to disable.
-w /usr/share/cmdscribe        -p wa -k cmdscribe_tamper
-w /etc/rsyslog.d/10-cmdscribe.conf -p wa -k cmdscribe_tamper
-w /var/log/cmdscribe.log      -p wa -k cmdscribe_tamper
-w /etc/audit/rules.d          -p wa -k audit_tamper

# Note on the log watch, measured rather than assumed: rsyslog opens the log once
# and appends through that descriptor, so ordinary writes do NOT generate events
# here -- which is good, because one audit record per logged command would bury
# the signal. What does fire is anything opening the file for writing: truncation,
# an editor, a `> file` redirect. That is the tamper event worth having.

# Common escalation paths and the files this project exists to catch.
-w /etc/sudoers                -p wa -k sudoers
-w /etc/sudoers.d/             -p wa -k sudoers
-w /root                       -p a   -k root_perms
-w /etc/crontab                -p wa  -k cron
-w /etc/cron.d/                -p wa  -k cron
